    <statement><p>Calculate the Taylor polynomials <m>T_2(x)</m> and <m>T_3(x)</m> centered at <m>x = 5</m> for <m>f(x) = \ln\mathopen{}\left(x+1\right)</m>.</p>
<p><m>T_2(x) =</m> <fillin characters="80" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>T_3(x) = T_2(x) +</m> <fillin characters="60" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>1.79176+0.166667\mathopen{}\left(x-5\right)-0.0138889\mathopen{}\left(x-5\right)^{2}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0.00154321\mathopen{}\left(x-5\right)^{3}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x</m> = a:
<me>T(x) = f(a) + \frac{f'(a)}{1!} (x-a) + \frac{f''(a)}{2!} (x-a)^2 + ... + \frac{f^n(a)}{n!} (x-a)^n</me>



<me>f(x) = \ln\mathopen{}\left(x+1\right)</me>
<me>f'(x) = \frac{1}{x+1}</me>
<me>f''(x) = \frac{-1}{\left(x+1\right)^{2}}</me>
<me>f'''(x) = \frac{2}{\left(x+1\right)^{3}}</me>



So, in this case, <m>f(x) = \ln\mathopen{}\left(x+1\right)</m> and 



<m>T_2(x) = A + B(x-5) + C(x-5)^2</m> 



where 



<m>A=f(5)=\ln (6)=1.79176</m> 



<m>B=f'(5)=\frac{1}{(6)}=0.166667</m> 



<m>C=\frac{f''(5)}{2!}=-\frac{1}{(72)}=-0.0138889</m> 



Thus, 



<me>T_2(x) = \ln (6) + \frac{1}{(6)} (x - 5)  - \frac{1}{(72)} (x - 5)^2</me>



or,



<me>T_2(x) = 1.79176+0.166667\mathopen{}\left(x-5\right)-0.0138889\mathopen{}\left(x-5\right)^{2}</me>



Similarly, 



<m>T_3(x) = D + E(x-5) + F(x-5)^2 + G(x-5)^3</m> 



where 



<m>D=f(5)=\ln (6)=1.79176</m> 



<m>E=f'(5)=\frac{1}{(6)}=0.166667</m> 



<m>F=\frac{f''(5)}{2!}=-\frac{1}{(72)}=-0.0138889</m> 



<m>G=\frac{f'''(5)}{3!}=\frac{1}{(648)}=0.00154321</m> 



Thus, 



<me>T_3(x) = \ln (6) + \frac{1}{(6)} (x - 5)  - \frac{1}{(72)} (x - 5)^2 + \frac{1}{(648)}  (x - 5)^3</me>



or,



<me>T_3(x) = T_2(x)  + 0.00154 (x-5)^3</me>





</solution>
